en that we have in Everett  Massachusetts and we've installed a  generator and you see a picture of it  there this is the first generator of its  kind that's being used in the United  States at least we're doing more of this  in the UK that's running completely from  straight cooking oil it's not it's not  being chemically processed into you know  a different kind of a fuel it's a  straight cooking oil and it's powering  our entire commissary facility so we got  a lot of really interesting press about  this and again you know we used to pay  to have this removed his waist now it's  it's a great source of power anaerobic  digestion being used a lot in Europe and  in other parts of the world not so much  in this country yet basically involves  taking food processing it without oxygen  it's basically composting but in an  environment that doesn't have oxygen and  so it uses the methane gas it captures  the methane gas and that's turned into  fuel or it's turned into power and by  the way at the end of the day there's  also a byproduct of compost so you're  getting at least two very valuable  streams out of food waste one of which  is power or electricity or fuel and one  of which is compost for agriculture  again this is an up-and-coming  technology we're aware of several  different projects locally in fact one  in Connecticut that you know are in the  initial stages of getting funding and  getting permitting we're hoping that  this is going to become a bigger  opportunity for for food waste a couple  of interesting examples of things that  are going on with some other byproducts  of food or agricultural waste the USDA  has some really interesting packaging  initiatives going on and a couple of  examples that I have seen and heard  about recently one of which is using  chicken feathers to make packaging  another which is using fish fins to make  packaging you know that couldn't begin  to explain to you how they do that and  what technologies are involved but  that's wh